# Process environment for the Android head, applied by @(AndroidEnvironment) in
# WPR.Platform.Android.csproj. Registered there, not here, so see that file for the wiring.
#
# --- Graphics ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FNA3D picks its graphics driver in FNA3D_PrepareWindowAttributes by walking `drivers[]` in order
# and taking the first whose PrepareWindowAttributes succeeds. On Android the compiled-in drivers
# are OpenGL then Vulkan, so without a force OpenGL is offered first.
#
# We force VULKAN, on the emulator and on hardware alike. Two reasons, and the first is the one
# that matters:
#
#   1. The OpenGL driver marshals every GPU call made off the device thread onto that thread and
#      BLOCKS the caller on a semaphore (ForceToMainThread, 19 call sites; Vulkan and D3D11 have
#      none). That queue is drained in exactly one place - ExecuteCommands, called from
#      OPENGL_SwapBuffers. A game whose loader thread creates a texture while the game thread is
#      blocked on a lock that loader holds deadlocks outright, with no crash and no CPU. Fable:
#      Coin Golf did precisely that, hanging forever on asset #33; on Vulkan it loads all 602 and
#      plays. That whole bug class does not exist off the GL driver.
#   2. Emulator and hardware then run the same driver, so an emulator result means something.
#
# THIS REVERSES A LONG-STANDING FORCE OF OpenGL, and the reason that force existed is now fixed
# properly rather than avoided. Mirror's Edge used to render its world correctly while every
# skinned character stayed in bind pose, and that was blamed on the Vulkan driver mistranslating
# SkinnedEffect's 72-entry float4x3 Bones array. It was not. Measured 2026-09-07:
#
#   * The SPIR-V is CORRECT - verified instruction by instruction for the one-bone and four-bone
#     variants, including the relative addressing, the x3 bone stride and the array bounds.
#   * The bone matrices arrive CORRECTLY, and animate.
#   * The real cause was VERTEX FORMATS. BlendIndices is Byte4, which FNA3D maps to
#     VK_FORMAT_R8G8B8A8_USCALED - optional in Vulkan for vertex buffers, and not implemented by
#     Adreno. The attribute delivered nothing, every bone index read 0, bone 0 is identity, and
#     the mesh rendered its bind pose. SHORT2 and SHORT4 are unsupported for the same reason.
#
# The fix is above the driver, in Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Graphics.VertexFormatExpansion, which
# rewrites those three formats to float. If you ever revert that, revert this too.
#
# FNA3D reads this through SDL_GetHint, which falls back to SDL_getenv - hence a real process
# environment variable rather than Environment.SetEnvironmentVariable, which .NET does not
# propagate to the native environ on Unix.
#
# Exact string matters: it is strcmp'd against FNA3D_Driver.Name, which is "Vulkan"
# (FNA3D_Driver_Vulkan.c). "vulkan" and "VK" silently match nothing.
#
# A per-device override still wins over this: fna3d_driver.txt next to the app's external files,
# containing OpenGL, Vulkan or auto. Use that to test a regression before changing this file.
FNA3D_FORCE_DRIVER=Vulkan

# --- Audio -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FAudio's SDL2 platform backend (FAudio_platform_sdl2.c) reads this to decide the rate it opens
# the output device at, and derives the mix buffer from it. Android's audio HAL runs at 48 kHz on
# essentially every device (confirmed on the emulator: media.audio_flinger reports "Sample rate:
# 48000 Hz"), so asking for anything else makes the platform insert an output resampler — extra
# CPU and another place for the 10 ms callback to miss its deadline.
#
# Note SDL_OpenAudioDevice is called with allowed_changes = 0 on Android (FAudio only sets
# SDL_AUDIO_ALLOW_SAMPLES_CHANGE for the pulseaudio workaround), so the requested rate is NOT
# negotiated down — it is worth getting right here rather than hoping.
#
# Pairs with FAUDIO_1024_QUANTUM in FnaAudioBackend: at 48 kHz that quantum is exactly 1024
# frames (21.33 ms). The transcoded soundtracks are 44.1 kHz, which FAudio resamples per source
# voice — that part is cheap and normal; it is the OUTPUT rate that wants to match the device.
SDL_AUDIO_FREQUENCY=48000

# --- Fallback ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The CAUTION above is now mitigated, not removed. SDL2_FNAPlatform.PrepareWindowAttributesWithFallback
# catches a forced-driver selection failure and retries once with automatic selection, so a device
# where OpenGL declines starts on Vulkan (T-posing characters) instead of not starting at all.
# Verified 2026-08-31 by setting this to a deliberately bogus name: logcat showed
#   "FNA3D_FORCE_DRIVER=NotADriver is not usable on this device. Retrying with automatic ..."
#   "FNA3D Driver: Vulkan"
# and the game ran, where before the same config died with InvalidOperationException.
#
# Trap if you touch that code: the failure arrives as a managed EXCEPTION, not a 0 return.
# FNALoggerEXT.FNA3DLogError throws InvalidOperationException from inside the native log callback,
# so FNA3D_PrepareWindowAttributes never returns on the failure path. A return-value check alone
# silently does nothing — that was the first attempt at this fix.
